linuxmuster-squid (7.3.4) lmn73; urgency=medium

  * Fix an intermittent failure of the package configuration, present since
    7.3.1: "chown: changing ownership of '/var/lib/linuxmuster-squid/
    instances/.git/objects/maintenance.lock': No such file or directory",
    after which dpkg reports that the post-installation script failed. The
    script committed the instance change log as root and changed the
    ownership afterwards, while git was still running its maintenance in the
    background (git 2.47 and later do that after a commit; Ubuntu 24.04's own
    git 2.43 does not, the git-core PPA's does). Ownership is now set first,
    every git command runs as the service user lmnsquid, and background
    maintenance is off for this repository, so no git process outlives the
    installation. On upgrade, files in the repository that belong to root
    (written by the old script, or by git commands an admin ran as root) are
    handed to lmnsquid before git runs; the change log keeps its history.
  * 7.3.3 was tagged but never released: the install check of its release
    run caught this failure. 7.3.4 contains all changes listed under 7.3.3.
    Admins who hit the chown error with 7.3.1 or 7.3.2 can finish that
    installation with `dpkg --configure -a`, or install 7.3.4 over it.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <mail@kevin-stenzel.de>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0200

linuxmuster-squid (7.3.3) lmn73; urgency=medium

  * Reproducible builds with verified dependencies. The Python packages in
    /opt/linuxmuster-squid/venv (and pip itself) now come from a lock file
    with SHA-256 hashes instead of "whatever PyPI serves at build time": two
    builds of the same version ship the same dependency versions, and a file
    whose hash does not match stops the build. Only prebuilt wheels are
    installed; nothing is compiled from source during the build. The build
    container, the GitHub Actions and the base of the data-plane image are
    pinned by digest; updates to any of them arrive only as reviewed
    Renovate pull requests.
  * The build only takes Python releases that have been public for at least
    a week. Two bundled libraries are therefore one release older than in
    7.3.2, whose versions were then only days old: idna 3.19 (was 3.20) and
    watchfiles 1.2.0 (was 1.3.0). Neither changes what the service does
    (watchfiles is only used by uvicorn's auto-reload, which is off; idna
    only for non-ASCII host names). All other library versions are those of
    7.3.2; configuration, service, CLI and upgrade path are unchanged.
  * Releases are created as drafts, get their .deb attached and are
    published only then, so a release never appears without its package.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <mail@kevin-stenzel.de>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 13:30:00 +0200

linuxmuster-squid (7.3.2) lmn73; urgency=medium

  * CI gains an `upgrade-smoke` job (ci.yml and release.yml): it installs the
    newest published release, gives it the state a real proxy host has (a
    keytab under secrets/, an admin-maintained file in the config directory, an
    instance with a pinned image, a blocklist entry), upgrades onto the freshly
    built package and fails the build if config.yml, the keytab, the admin file
    or a blocklist survives with different bytes or different permissions, if
    any admin-set key of the instance record changed (compared as a positive
    list of keys, so the record may still gain fields), if the service is no
    longer enabled+active, if the CLI no longer reports the .deb version, if
    anything is left behind under /opt or is world-writable, or if the instance
    change log recorded nothing. `release.yml` will not publish a tag whose
    upgrade smoke failed.
  * The upgrade is installed with `--reinstall --allow-downgrades`, so the
    postinst really runs with its `$2` set and the upgrade branch
    (try-restart, update-all, reconcile) is exercised instead of silently
    skipped when the build is version-equal to the release. That it really
    happened is asserted, not assumed: dpkg's own log must carry an `upgrade`
    action for the package, and the checksum manifest may not run empty.
  * The job refuses to skip itself when the package has releases: it falls back
    to the newest prerelease, and only a package without any release at all may
    skip. It gates the release job, so a silent skip would publish a tag whose
    upgrade path was never tested.
  * `workflow_dispatch` takes a `prev_tag` input so the jump from an arbitrary
    older release (e.g. v1.3.0, the pre-conventions line) can be proven on
    demand.
  * ci.yml only runs on pushes to `main` and tags; every other branch is
    covered by its pull request, which halves the CI runs per commit.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <mail@kevin-stenzel.de>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 19:20:00 +0200

linuxmuster-squid (7.3.1) lmn73; urgency=medium

  * The domain blocklist gets a product path (it was reachable only from the
    e2e compose stack): every instance owns
    /etc/linuxmuster-squid/blocklists/<name>/blocked.domains, mounted read-only
    into its container at /etc/squid/lists, the file the rendered squid.conf
    already reads. New `lmnsquid blocklist <name> list|add <domain>|remove
    <domain>|reload` (GET/POST/DELETE /v1/instances/{name}/blocklist,
    POST .../blocklist/reload). `add` stores `.example.org` = the domain and all
    subdomains; `reload` runs `squid -k reconfigure` inside the container -- no
    restart, no cache loss (not a docker kill signal: Docker treats any kill as a
    manual stop and would not restart the container after a reboot). A blocked
    HTTP request gets 403; a blocked HTTPS host is cut at the TLS handshake
    (peek + terminate), the browser shows a connection error, not a block page.
  * Upgrade from 7.3.0: containers created before 7.3.1 run without the mount
    until they are replaced once. The postinst runs `lmnsquid reconcile` for
    that when the previous version is < 7.3.1 (a few seconds per instance);
    run it yourself if the postinst could not.
  * Replacing a container (create, edit, update, rollback, reconcile) no longer
    has a gap for failure: the new container is created first, the old one is
    kept until the new one is healthy and restarted if it is not, and the
    operation reports the error instead of leaving the instance without a
    container. A container whose definition is unchanged is left alone (label
    lmnsquid.spec), so `reconcile` touches only what differs. One instance
    failing no longer stops `reconcile` for the others (`failed` in the API
    result); `lmnsquid reconcile` and `update-all` exit 1 when an instance was
    not brought up or rolled back. The postinst logs both steps to the journal
    (`journalctl -t linuxmuster-squid`) and prints a WARNING in the apt output
    on failure instead of discarding it.
  * blocklist-refresh.sh writes the instance's list under
    /etc/linuxmuster-squid/blocklists/$INSTANCE/ by default and tells you to
    `lmnsquid blocklist <name> reload`; it replaces the whole file.
  * CI: the built package must carry an md5sums entry for every shipped file
    and `dpkg --verify` must be silent after the install smoke.
  * `lmnsquid rm` now removes the instance's cache and log volumes (the access
    log is personal data), its blocklist and the updater's .prev note;
    `--keep-logs` keeps the log volume (DELETE ...?keep_logs=true).
  * The git change log of /var/lib/linuxmuster-squid/instances works. The
    postinst configured the repository after handing it to lmnsquid, git refused
    ("dubious ownership") and no commit was ever written. It now initialises
    and configures the repository first, adds safe.directory for root, commits
    definitions left uncommitted by 7.3.0 on upgrade, and the store logs git
    failures at ERROR instead of debug (author identity passed explicitly).
  * Ship the admin scripts the docs refer to under
    /usr/share/linuxmuster-squid/scripts/ (provision-keytab.sh,
    discover-ad-facts.sh, blocklist-refresh.sh).
  * discover-ad-facts.sh lists the global role groups role-teacher/role-student/
    role-staff and the per-school internet groups; it matched every class and
    project group ending in -teachers/-students before.
  * The .deb carries md5sums, so `dpkg --verify linuxmuster-squid` checks it.
  * docs: new docs/install.md with the procedure tested against a real
    linuxmuster.net 7.3 domain (service account + keytab via
    provision-keytab.sh, one instance per role, group names, checks, blocklist,
    update, removal); keytab-and-dns.md names the account commands (incl.
    setexpiry --noexpiry); architecture, threat model, test strategy and the
    GPO acceptance list describe the blocklist as shipped.

 -- Kevin Stenzel <mail@kevin-stenzel.de>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 15:00:00 +0200

linuxmuster-squid (7.3.0) lmn73; urgency=medium

  * Switch to the linuxmuster.net version scheme (7.<line>.<n>, distribution lmn73).
  * debian/changelog is the single version source: the .deb, the Python package,
    `lmnsquid version` and GET /v1/version all report it (they reported 0.4.0 on
    1.3.x packages).
  * Ship /usr/bin/lmnsquid so the CLI is on PATH right after apt install (no
    manual symlink).
  * Security fix for servers upgraded from 1.3.0: the postinst now clears the
    group/world write bits under /opt/linuxmuster-squid on every configure.
    1.3.0 shipped that tree world-writable; an upgrade reset the files but left
    336 directories at 0777 -- among them the venv's site-packages, where any
    local user could drop a .pth file that then executes as lmnsquid, who is in
    the docker group. Fresh 1.3.0-free installs were never affected.
  * Internetsperre bites within ~10s instead of ~30s: the internet-group ACL
    cache drops from ttl=30 negative_ttl=15 to ttl=10 negative_ttl=10 (grace=0
    unchanged, live LDAP). Needs the new image (`lmnsquid update-all`);
    DEFAULT_IMAGE is pinned to it.
  * Add `make deb`; CI builds the package in ghcr.io/linuxmuster/lmndev-runner:24.04
    and install-smokes it on ubuntu-24.04; a tag v<version> releases it after a
    tag == changelog gate (replaces build-deb.yml).
  * Unify the maintainer address to Kevin Stenzel <mail@kevin-stenzel.de>.
  * Pin the ruff rule set to the classic defaults (E4/E7/E9/F) and the CI tools.
  * docs: the GPO deployment recipe binds the proxy ACL to the global role groups
    (role-teacher/role-student) plus one internet group per school; the E2E
    proves the internet gate (13/13).

